/*!

\page overview_lightweight_quadrature Overview of the lightweight_quadrature package
\version Draco-@Draco_VERSION@
\author Jim Warsa

\section lightweight_quadrature_abstract Abstract

The lightweight_quadrature package provides 2D quadrature on the sphere, normalized to 4 pi.
They include multiple-range quadratures, in addition to good ol' level symmetric and
gauss-Legendre-Chebyshev in both product and triangular arrangements.

In contrast to the quadrature package, this does nothing fancy and provide no additional services,
returing only a list of 3D points and associate quadrature weights. 

The following 1D polar and azimuthal types of quadratures are available. 

-------------------------------------------
      1D polar           1D azimuthal
-------------------------------------------
     gauss                chebyshev
     double-gauss
     lobatto              az_qrs45
     radau                az_qra45
     double-radau         az_qrs30
                          az_qra30
     pol_qrs90            az_qrs90
     pol_qrs45            az_qra90
-------------------------------------------
recommended product quadratures (no closed 1D polar quadratures for now)
-------------------------------------------
      polar              azimuthal
   (0) gauss           (0) chebyshev
   (1) double-gauss

   (3) qrs90           (1) az_qrs45
   (3) qrs45           (2) az_qra45
                     (3) az_qrs30
                       (4) az_qra30
                       (5) az_qrs90
                       (6) az_qra90
-------------------------------------------
recommended triangular quadratures (based on integration accuracy)
-------------------------------------------
      polar              azimuthal
   (0) gauss           (0) chebyshev
   (1) double-gauss

   (3) qrs90           (2) az_qra45
   (3) qrs45           (5) az_qrs90

<!---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- -->

\section lightweight_quadrature_usage Usage

\code

#include "lightweight_quadrature/quad_2D.hh"

int polar_order = 4;
int azimuhtal_order = 4;
int polar_type = 0;
int azimuthal_type = 0;

std::vector<double> omega_x;
std::vector<double> omega_y; 
std::vector<double> omega_z; 
std::vector<double> weights;
auto const quad = quad_2D(polar_order, azimuthal_order, polar_type, azimuthal_type, quad_type);
quad.create_quadrature(omega_x_, omega_y_, omega_z_, weights_);

\endcode

<!---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- -->

\section lightweight_quadrature_reference References

\li P.J. Spence, "The generation of arbitrary order, non-classical, Gauss-type quadrature for 
transport applications", Journal of Computational Physics, Volume 296, 2015, Pages 25-57.

*/

